description:
LOCATION: crate-jmap-contacts-server/src/backend.rs:35-49

DOC:
    /// Create a new AddressBook or ContactCard.
    ///
    /// Returns `(assigned_id, created_object)` on success. `create_id`
    /// is the client-side creation id used in the `/set` request. Per-
    /// request auth context is available via the `caller` parameter,
    /// which the `register_contacts_handlers` closures forward unchanged
    /// from [`jmap_server::Dispatcher::dispatch`].
    fn create_object<O: SetObject + Send + Sync>(
        ...
        obj: O,
    ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<(Id, O), BackendSetError<Self::Error>>> + Send;

PROBLEM:
The doc does not say what the backend MUST do with `obj` before
storing it. The handler at addressbook.rs:122-138 / card.rs:103-110
injects a `"placeholder"` string into the `id` field of `obj_val` so
that the typed deserialize succeeds — the trait impl is REQUIRED to
overwrite this placeholder with the server-minted id before storing,
or the store will contain a card with `id == "placeholder"` and every
subsequent /get / /set lookup will fail.

The reference impl at memory.rs:612-622 does the right thing:
    let server_id = Self::demo_next_id(&mut inner, O::TYPE_NAME, ...);
    let mut val = serde_json::to_value(&obj)?;
    if let Some(map) = val.as_object_mut() {
        map.insert("id".to_owned(), Value::String(server_id.as_ref().to_owned()));
    }

…but a backend implementor reading ONLY the ContactsBackend doc
comment will not know:
- That `obj.id` is a meaningless placeholder.
- That they MUST overwrite `obj.id` before storage.
- That they MUST return the rewritten obj as the second tuple element.

The canonical sibling jmap-mail-server/src/backend.rs at "create_object"
docs this sentinel-field contract explicitly (per the explore subagent's
inspection: "Missing the mail-canonical 'Sentinel fields the backend
MUST replace' subsection"). Contacts diverged without justification.

This is JMAP-qz9v.13's broader trait-doc analog. JMAP-qz9v.13 flags the
specific placeholder mechanic in the handler; this finding flags that
the trait contract itself omits the rule that makes the placeholder
mechanic work.

WHAT BACKEND IMPLEMENTORS CANNOT DEPEND ON:
- That `obj.id` is the id they should store under.
- That returning `obj` unchanged is correct.
- That the handler will retry/repair if they store a placeholder id.

EVIDENCE OF CONFUSION RISK:
The MockBackend in lib.rs:386-394 returns the input obj unchanged:
    async fn create_object<O: SetObject + Send + Sync>(...) -> ... {
        Ok((Id::from("mock-id-1"), obj))
    }
This silently ships a card with the original (placeholder) obj.id
even after returning a different Id from the tuple. The MockBackend
shape suggests the contract is "return any id and the object as-is";
the MemoryBackend reference impl says "rewrite id, then return".
These disagree. A new backend implementor has two divergent canonical
examples in the same crate.

RECOMMENDED:
Document the contract on ContactsBackend::create_object. Minimum:

    /// # Server-assigned fields
    ///
    /// `obj.id` arrives carrying a placeholder; the backend MUST
    /// overwrite it with the server-minted id (returned as the tuple's
    /// first element) before storing, and the returned `O` MUST reflect
    /// the server-minted id. Failing to overwrite produces a record
    /// that subsequent /get and /set calls cannot reach.
    ///
    /// Other server-set fields (timestamps, default flags, etc.) MAY
    /// be added to the returned `O` and the handler will surface them
    /// in the /set response per RFC 8620 §5.3.

PROPAGATION: this is a sibling-of-canonical cookie-cutter regression;
align the doc with jmap-mail-server's contract.
